# Target C — audited §165 Type-I diameter-cover spare-capacity criterion

**Date:** 2026-09-12
**Lifecycle:** audited-working proof-only route module; theorem-state authority unchanged.
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

This module normalizes the hostile-audited diameter-vertex-cover repacking theorem and
extends it, using §162, from an internal inactive chamber to **either** Type-I
macroscopic inactive container: an internal chamber or an exterior cap.

The key point is that one need not find a triangle-free one-extra-point exchange.
If a protected inactive container can absorb its current isolate population plus a
vertex cover of the **complete actual diameter graph**, then deleting that cover removes
all old diameter pairs and repacking deep inside the old source hull creates no new
ones. The resulting same-cardinality unit-separated source has strict diameter below
`D(n)`, a direct contradiction.

---

## 165.1 Protected packing capacity

Let `C_*` be the Type-I macroscopic inactive container supplied by §162, internal or
exterior, and let `Z_*` be its assigned contact-isolated source set. Put

\[
z_*:=|Z_*|.
\]

Every source point not assigned to `Z_*` lies outside `int(C_*)`, and for every fixed
positive depth the inner parallel body exists for all sufficiently large `D`.
Define

\[
C_{*,-1}:=\{x\in C_*:\operatorname{dist}(x,\partial C_*)>1\}
\]

and its protected unit-packing capacity

\[
\boxed{
M_1(C_*):=\sup\Bigl\{|Y|:
Y\subset C_{*,-1},\ |y-y'|\ge1\ (y\ne y')\Bigr\}.
}
\tag{165.1}
\]

For every `y in C_{*,-1}` and every retained source point `a notin int(C_*)`, convexity
implies

\[
|y-a|>1:
\]

the segment from `y` to `a` meets `partial C_*` only after traveling more than one
unit.

Moreover `C_{*,-1} subset int(C_*) subset int(K)`, where `K=conv(A)` is the old full
source hull. The last inclusion holds for both container types: an interior point of a
positive-area convex subset of `K` cannot lie on a supporting line of `K`.

---

## 165.2 Deleting a diameter vertex cover gives strict global descent

Let

\[
H=(B,E_H)
\]

be the **complete actual diameter graph** supplied by C65, and let `U subset B` be any
vertex cover of `H`.

Suppose

\[
\boxed{M_1(C_*)\ge z_*+|U|.}
\tag{165.2}
\]

Choose a unit-separated set

\[
Y\subset C_{*,-1},
\qquad |Y|=z_*+|U|,
\]

and define

\[
A':=(A\setminus(Z_*\cup U))\cup Y.
\tag{165.3}
\]

Then

\[
\boxed{
|A'|=|A|,
\qquad
\min_{x\ne y\in A'}|x-y|\ge1,
\qquad
\operatorname{diam}A'<D.
}
\tag{165.4}
\]

### Proof

Cardinality is exact because `Z_*` and `U` are disjoint: `Z_*` consists of
contact-isolated inactive points, while `U` consists of loaded endpoints of actual
diameter pairs.

Retained old-old pairs remain unit-separated. New-new pairs are unit-separated by the
choice of `Y`. Every retained old point lies outside `int(C_*)`, so the protected-depth
argument above gives strict new-old distance greater than one.

Because `U` covers the complete actual diameter graph, every retained old-old pair has
distance strictly below `D`.

Every new point lies in `int(K)`. For any `x in K` and `y in int(K)`,

\[
|x-y|<D.
\tag{165.5}
\]

Indeed `K subset \overline B(x,D)` for each `x in K`, since `diam(K)=D`; an interior
point of `K` cannot lie on the boundary circle of that disk. Thus every new-old and
new-new distance is strictly below `D`.

All pair distances in the finite set `A'` are therefore strictly below `D`, so

\[
\operatorname{diam}A'<D=D(n),
\]

contradicting global minimality. \(\square\)

No triangle-free condition on `A'` is needed, and no contact count or KKT force is
compared. The contradiction is direct diameter descent.

---

## 165.3 Woodall gives a half-terminal cover

Let

\[
q:=|V(H)|.
\]

The canonical Woodall classification gives two possibilities for the actual diameter
graph.

### Forest branch

If `H` is a forest, it is bipartite. The smaller color class is a vertex cover, so

\[
\tau(H)\le\left\lfloor {q\over2}\right\rfloor.
\tag{165.6}
\]

### Connected odd-unicyclic branch

Suppose the unique cycle has length `2k+1` and every remaining loaded vertex is a
pendant leaf attached directly to a cycle vertex. Let `L` be the number of pendant
leaves.

If `L=0`, then

\[
\tau(H)=k+1
=\left\lceil{2k+1\over2}\right\rceil.
\tag{165.7}
\]

This bare odd-cycle case is separated explicitly; the strict averaging inequality used
below requires `L>0`.

If `L>0`, let `ell_i` be the number of leaves at cycle vertex `i`. Among the `2k+1`
maximum independent sets of size `k` in the odd cycle, each cycle vertex occurs in
exactly `k` sets. Hence the average attached-leaf weight of such an independent set is

\[
{k\over2k+1}L<{L\over2}.
\tag{165.8}
\]

Choose one maximum independent set `I` with leaf weight below `L/2`. Take as a vertex
cover all cycle vertices outside `I` together with all leaves attached to vertices of
`I`. Its size is at most

\[
\left\lceil {q\over2}\right\rceil.
\]

Thus in every Woodall branch

\[
\boxed{
\tau(H)\le\left\lceil {q\over2}\right\rceil.
}
\tag{165.9}
\]

The loaded vertices occur in convex position and are mutually unit-separated around
the force hull, so

\[
q\le\operatorname{per}(F)\le\pi D.
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\tau(H)
\le\left\lceil {q\over2}\right\rceil
\le {\pi D\over2}+1.
}
\tag{165.10}
\]

---

## 165.4 Exact Type-I survivor inequality

Taking `U` to be a minimum vertex cover in §165.2 gives the necessary survivor
condition

\[
\boxed{
M_1(C_*)\le z_*+\tau(H)-1.
}
\tag{165.11}
\]

Equivalently, define the protected spare capacity

\[
\operatorname{spare}(C_*):=M_1(C_*)-z_*.
\]

Every Type-I survivor must satisfy

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{spare}(C_*)<\tau(H)
\le\left\lceil {q\over2}\right\rceil.
}
\tag{165.12}
\]

Hence either Type-I container branch closes as soon as one proves the **lower bound**

\[
\boxed{
M_1(C_*)-z_*\ge\tau(H).
}
\tag{165.13}
\]

A topology-free sufficient condition is

\[
\boxed{
M_1(C_*)-z_*
\ge\left\lceil {q\over2}\right\rceil.
}
\tag{165.14}
\]

The direction in (165.13)–(165.14) is binding. The hostile audit of the historical
source corrected an erroneous final prose sentence that had described the desired
spare-capacity estimate with the opposite inequality direction. Since
`tau(H)<=pi D/2+O(1)`, at most that many extra protected slots are required, but the
theorem to prove is a **lower bound** on the number of available slots.

---

## 165.5 Baseline lattice lower bound and exact residual gap

Let `Lambda` be the unit triangular lattice, with covolume

\[
a_0={\sqrt3\over2}.
\]

Averaging

\[
|(t+\Lambda)\cap C_{*,-1}|
\]

over one translation fundamental domain gives mean

\[
{\operatorname{area}(C_{*,-1})\over a_0}.
\]

Therefore some translate gives

\[
\boxed{
M_1(C_*)
\ge
\left\lceil{\operatorname{area}(C_{*,-1})\over a_0}\right\rceil.
}
\tag{165.15}
\]

This baseline does **not** by itself prove (165.13). The canonical container may have a
linear boundary-order packing surplus/slack. Thus the exact Type-I frontier is now a
linear protected packing-capacity problem:

> prove that a macroscopic near-capacity convex inactive packing has at least
> `tau(H)` spare unit-packing slots in its depth-one inner core, or prove a sharper
> topology-dependent lower bound sufficient for the actual Woodall diameter graph.

This target is stronger and cleaner than producing independent first-contact motions:
once (165.13) holds, all actual diameter pairs disappear simultaneously and no
triangle-free comparison-class condition remains.

---

## Scope audit

- C65 is essential: `U` covers the complete actual diameter graph, not merely one
  selected positive KKT diameter support.
- The exterior-cap extension uses §162's protected-depth theorem. No new assumption on
  cap containment is introduced.
- Every deleted isolate is replaced one-for-one; the additional `|U|` replacement
  slots correspond exactly to the deleted loaded diameter-cover vertices.
- New points lie strictly inside the old full source hull and therefore create no
  distance-`D` pairs.
- Fixed depth `>1` protects unit separation from every retained source outside the
  selected container interior.
- Triangle-freeness of the competitor is intentionally unnecessary.
- Contact-isolated coordinates never enter the Section-57 core affine kernel.
- The bare odd-cycle `L=0` case is handled separately before the leaf-weight averaging
  argument.
- The lattice bound is only a baseline; no unsupported linear boundary gain is claimed.

**Disposition:** §165 PASS at audited-working proof-only scope. It reduces both Type-I
macroscopic-container alternatives to the single quantitative spare-capacity condition
(165.13). C-II and Level I remain open.
